age":{"input_tokens":55637,"output_tokens":2328,"requests":3}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"941c126623caefff059184e0bf8dc87ea492e54c0bff8b91297d037ecd090ae2"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T3","usage":{"input_tokens":261274,"output_tokens":3362,"requests":8}}],"version":1},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Retire idle OLD containers with `docker stop <name>` (SIGTERM → the container's own bounded in_flight self-drain in _mcp_health.run_http_with_grace, covered by stop_grace_period=1260s) rather than `docker rm -f`, so retirement leans on the already-built graceful drain and closes the poll→kill TOCTOU; reserve `rm -f` only for the RETIRE_CAP-exceeded reporting path (which emits the marker instead of killing).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.2889,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan step 6: 'Retire the OLD container, gated by a still-serving? check on its /health in_flight gauge … remove older ones ONLY when idle; NEVER kill in_flight>0'","Sweep uses `docker rm -f` per Run mechanism / AC ('no docker rm of it')","src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:170-177 make_sigterm_handler: server.should_exit=True then drain_then_exit(grace_seconds…) — the container drains in_flight itself on SIGTERM","docker-compose.yml:325-329 stop_grace_period: 1260s comment: 'a retiring container waits for an in-flight certified op to finish'; must stay >= DEFAULT_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_SECONDS=1200","`docker rm -f` sends SIGKILL, not the SIGTERM the self-drain handler is bound to"],"finding":"The OLD-container retire step reinvents the drain gate with `docker rm -f` (SIGKILL, no grace) gated only by an external /health in_flight poll, when the MCP container already implements a bounded, tested SIGTERM self-drain (src/rebar/_mcp_health.py run_http_with_grace: stop intake, wait up to 1200s for in_flight→0) that compose stop_grace_period=1260s covers. `docker rm -f` bypasses that self-drain and creates a check-then-act race: the poll can read idle while an op is completing, then SIGKILL it. Using `docker stop <name>` (SIGTERM) would delegate to the container's own graceful drain and eliminate the TOCTOU.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7efaca68ed5b18c9","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 6 (Retire the OLD container) + Run mechanism (mcp_retire_sweep)","norm_id":"n207a967758792a60","priority":0.8889,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An OLD container's in_flight transitions 1→0 as an op finishes; the sweep polls 0 and rm -f SIGKILLs the process while the op's final store/opcert write is landing","A retired OLD container is idle at poll time but an already-connected client op resumes between poll and rm -f"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Change step 6 to: after the flip, `docker stop` (not `rm -f`) each retired OLD container — its SIGTERM handler drains in_flight up to its own grace before exiting, so no external in_flight poll or hard kill is needed for correctness; the poll becomes an optimization/telemetry input, and the RETIRE_CAP guard still emits AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP rather than force-killing when the pool is full.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"high","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"high","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State how retirement handles the `--restart always` policy on the OLD container (e.g. `docker stop`/`rm` removes the container so the policy no longer applies), so a draining-then-exiting container cannot be resurrected onto its A/B port and corrupt the port-pool == cap accounting.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.501,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Run mechanism: 'flags reproduce the compose mcp: service EXACTLY — … --restart always'","docker-compose.yml:324 mcp restart: always","Plan does not address the restart-policy vs retire interaction (absence)"],"finding":"The plan specifies `docker run --restart always` for the NEW container but does not state how a RETIRING container's `--restart always` policy interacts with retirement: if the retiring process exits on its own during the drain window, Docker's restart policy would resurrect it, and the sweep must still reclaim it deterministically.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7a7ec71d5b7042ef","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Run mechanism (mcp_run_new flags) + Approach step 6","norm_id":"n314d6d865ab43a18","priority":0.099,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A retired OLD container that has finished draining exits, `--restart always` restarts it, and the next sweep re-observes it as a managed container occupying its A/B port"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","